{"number": 156, "text": "           53.3\n                      d\n\n                      e\n                      b\n                      a\n                      f\n\n\n           53.4\n                   Possible answers:\n                   1  Professor Ward has given a wonderful lecture. Please give her a big hand.\n                   2  | know I have one somewhere, but | can’t lay my hands on it at the moment.\n                   3  | think we should all act and do something, not just sit on our hands.\n                   4  |’m ready to hand over the reins to someone else.\n                   5  Hand on heart, have you never told a lie?\n\n                   Unit  54\n\n          54.1\n                   1  b)       of gold\n                   2  b) No. It is often used ironically.\n                   3  b) sobbing\n                   4 a) your general well-being. In some contexts, it might also be used with reference to someone’s\n                        financial interests.\n                   5 a) suffering\n                   6 b) unpleasant\n\n          54.2\n                   I’ve been offered a job that all my friends and family think | should take. The problem is that in my\n                   heart of hearts, | really don’t want it. It’s always been my heart’s desire to leave this city and work\n                   abroad, but | don’t have the heart to tell my family this, as | know be upset. | know you will tell\n                   me to take heart and be strong, but | really don’t want to hurt them. After all, | know they have my\n                   best interests at heart. What should | do?\n\n          54.3\n                   1 Don’t expect so much of him - he’s only four! Have a heart!\n                   2 You'll only get hurt if you wear your heart on your sleeve.\n                   3 His heart was in his boots as he thought about the difficult week ahead.\n                  4  You know  your parents have your best interests at heart.\n   